Q: Is there a global approach to catch network errors in javascript A: <p>There isn't native global solution.</p>
<p>I did this workaround, to make such errors bubble, then catching them in another script &quot;globally&quot;.<br />
Apply fix. Retries, alternative src ...<br />
I am including a bit more than asked for, it might be useful.</p>
<p>HTMLElement has an error event. That can fire on various errors. Also Network errors:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>when a resource failed to load, or can't be used. For example, if a script has an execution error or an image can't be found or is invalid.<br />
<a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLElement/error_event" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLElement/error_event</a></p>
</blockquote>
<p>They don't bubble. So I insert a proxy to each element I am interested in, also a callback which would replace the URL:</p>
<pre><code>var elImg = document.createElement('IMG');
elImg.src = __URL__;
elImg.addEventListener('error', evt =&gt; evt.target.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('resourcefailed', {bubbles: true,
        detail: value =&gt; elImg.src = value})));
</code></pre>
<p>Then wherever else catch those errors:</p>
<pre><code>triedAlternative = [];
document.addEventListener('resourcefailed', async evt =&gt; {
    if (triedAlternative.includes(e.g.SOME_ID_FROM_evt)) {
        console.error('Resource failed after retries.', evt);
        return;
    }
    triedAlternative.push(e.g.SOME_ID_FROM_evt);
    evt.detail(ALTERNATIVE_URL_VALUE);
});
</code></pre>
<p>It's important to remember to not create an endless loop, so I included the <code>triedAlternative</code> and <code>if</code> block.</p>
<p>Safety should be considered. e.g. I would not recommend to use this on a &lt;script&gt; element, unless the fixing function is isolated. Also a global counter with a limit.</p>
